[
    {
        "content": "<p>Would it be possible to teach <code>grind</code> to run auto-params of lemmas it applies? Or does it run into the same issue as <code>rw</code> and <code>simp</code> (in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/3475\">lean4#3475</a>)?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">grind</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trace_state</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">grind</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- fails</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 595157804,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1778789225
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For <code>simp</code> we can work around it by using a discharger that runs auto-params (as a temporary workaround), which is done in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/39333\">#39333</a>.</p>",
        "id": 595158118,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1778789351
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe this is beside the point, but it seems like <code>grind</code> will at least try to prove the autoparam itself, e.g. <code>grind [P]</code> works here.</p>",
        "id": 595343864,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1778861684
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sure. In my actual use case I want to be able to run a specialized tactic, that is maybe stronger than grind on particular problems. <code>fun_prop</code> comes into mind, since it's able to interpret a function as a composition of multiple more basic functions. This kind of \"higher-order\" reasoning (in the sense of higher-order unification) is a weakness of grind (I believe), and I could imagine we want to write more specialized tactics that would help with this.</p>",
        "id": 595348400,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1778862946
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I completely agree. Relevant to the grind set discussion as well, because even these new tactics that are really just using <code>grind</code> won't be picked up.</p>",
        "id": 595352698,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1778864212
    }
]